**9:15** And the four angels were loosed — the ones who had been made ready — they were loosed into the weight of the loosing, because the preparation had consumed them before the work. The hour and the day and the month and the year became their bones, and the bones were dried out by the waiting, and the waiting was dried out by the waiting. They were loosed therefore into themselves; this was the loosing, that the bound thing fell not from the bond but from the very being-bound, as dry wood falls from its own weight, not from wind. The third of mankind — but the third was not outside them; it was the portion of each one already dead in the preparing, living only in the being-counted, as a root lives in the earth only because the earth does not release it. And the angels, having been loosed, knew that that third was what they themselves had always been — the killing was the knowing, and the knowing was completed in the loosing itself, not after it.

**9:16** And the number of the armies — twice ten thousand times ten thousand — but the one who heard the number heard the emptiness of the number, because only the one who has lost being one is able to count; for the one who counts has already been divided into the thing counted, and the one divided is no longer the one counting. And there was no name for this multitude, because multitude comes to be where the one has died and its death did not cease. And I saw the horses in the vision — but the vision itself was their breastplate; the seeing clothed them with fire and with hyacinth and with sulfur, not because the horses had color but because the eye gave what it feared. For fear takes on the color of the one seeing, and the color taken becomes the skin of the thing seen;

**9:17** hence the breastplate — it was not fashioned but was looked into existence. And the heads of the horses were as the heads of those who had seen them, because the beast carries the face of the trembling one, and the trembling one sees himself in that which he trembles at and trembles the more. And the fire proceeding out of their mouths was the tongue that spoke it; the smoke the thing after the word, when the word departs and there remains only what it burned; and the sulfur the honor of saying what no one had asked to hear. These three — fire, smoke, sulfur — were one; the word and the thing after the word and the honor of the word, because every word kills something in the saying of it, and that dead thing was the life of the portion that was not said.
